' Conditions
' Comparisons, logic, and the conditional expression.

' A comparison answers 1 or 0. There is no separate boolean type: SMath
' worksheets compute with comparisons as numbers, and this language has to be
' able to receive them.
span = 6 m
allowed = 4 m
too_long = span > allowed

' Both sides are held in base SI, so a comparison converts before it compares.
' Neither of these needs a conversion written into it.
imperial = 1 in < 1 m
strong = 1 kip > 1 lbf

' `and`, `or` and `not` are words. `!` is factorial to anyone who has met SMath
' and `&` is bitwise to anyone who has met C, so neither is borrowed here.
in_range = span > 1 m and span < 10 m
outside = not in_range

' The conditional is an expression, so it composes with arithmetic and needs no
' statement form of its own.
overhang = if span > allowed then span - allowed else 0 m

' Only the arm that is taken is evaluated, which is what lets a conditional
' guard something that would otherwise fail. `bay[0]` would be out of bounds.
bay = [3 m, 4 m, 5 m]
n = 0
chosen = if n > 0 then bay[n] else 0 m

' Chained without brackets: the `else` arm reaches as far as it can, so `else
' if` is just another conditional inside it.
load = 7 kN
grade = if load < 5 kN then 1 else if load < 10 kN then 2 else 3

' A function body can be piecewise.
fn stress(f, a) = if a > 0 m^2 then f/a else 0 Pa
sigma = stress(10 kN, 2 m^2)

' Substitution shows which way the worksheet went. The arm that did not run is
' shown as it was written, because it has no values to show.
margin = if too_long then span - allowed else allowed - span

' A verdict in words. A string is a value like any other — it binds to a name,
' an `if` chooses between two, and `==` compares them — and it has no arithmetic
' at all, which is the whole of what a worksheet does with one.
a = 3 m
a_max = 4 m
verdict = if a <= a_max then "singly reinforced" else "doubly reinforced"
timber = "C24"
is_c24 = timber == "C24"
